<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gil Gavbara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gil Gavbara was a prince from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> who, according to later tradition, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> around 642 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> collapsed. He claimed Sasanian descent. His sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dabuya">Dabuya</a> and Paduspan founded the Dabuyid and Paduspanid dynasties, which kept Iranian rule alive on the Caspian coast long after the Arab conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gil-gavbara.jpg" alt="Gil Gavbara" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GilGavbaraHistoryofIran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
